    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In a direct democracy the citizens vote on laws and policies themselves, in an open assembly or a referendum, rather than leaving them to elected representatives.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from 508 to 322 BCE is the classic case: an assembly of male citizens, a council and juries chosen by lot governed, while women, slaves and resident foreigners had no vote.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> keeps the tradition alive in two cantonal open-air assemblies and in frequent national referendums and initiatives, devices also used by many US states.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At the open-air <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> of Appenzell Innerrhoden men traditionally carry a sword as proof of their right to vote; women were admitted only after the Federal Supreme Court ruled in 1990 that the canton must let them vote.</p></aside>
    </section>

